\chapter{Unix as Infrastructure}
\label{ch:infrastructure}
\index{system administration}\index{servers}\index{containers}\index{security}

\section{The user may never see the machine}

A person browsing a website may depend on Unix-like systems without
interacting with a terminal, desktop, or recognizable operating-system
interface. Servers perform computation behind a network protocol.
The user's experience is shaped by latency, availability, and correctness,
while the operating system recedes into an infrastructure layer.

This is not a complete break with the original system.
Time-sharing also mediated access to shared resources. The difference is
scale, distance, automation, and the division of responsibility.
A server may be operated by one organization, hosted by another, built
from hundreds of projects, and used by people who cannot inspect its
configuration or choose when it is upgraded.

Unix's process model, networking, toolchain, and scriptable environment
made it well suited to this setting. Free software made extensive
adaptation and redistribution possible. Commodity hardware improved the
economics. None of these advantages eliminates the work of keeping a
service dependable.

\section{Booting is the beginning of a dependency graph}
\index{init}\index{systemd}\index{service supervision}

After firmware and a bootloader arrange initial execution, the kernel
initializes essential facilities and starts user-space work.
The first user-space process in the traditional model has special
responsibilities for the rest of the environment. Historical init
arrangements and scripts established services in a largely sequential
style, with system-specific conventions.

Modern service managers can express dependencies, monitor processes,
restart failed services, collect logs, and coordinate sockets and other
resources. Systemd is a prominent Linux example; other systems and
communities use different supervisors. It is not a universal component
of Unix, nor is its presence a definition of Linux.

Supervision addresses a real gap between launching a process and
maintaining a service. A process may start successfully but fail to become
ready. A service may need a network mount, credentials, or a local database
before it can do useful work. Restarting it blindly can amplify a failure
or destroy diagnostic evidence.

Dependencies also contain cycles and ambiguous readiness conditions.
``The network is up'' can mean a configured interface, a route, successful
name resolution, or access to a particular service. A useful service
contract states the condition it actually needs. Administration becomes
more reliable when vague global milestones are replaced with specific
observable requirements.

\section{Permissions are a beginning, not a complete sandbox}
\index{permissions}\index{privilege separation}\index{SELinux}\index{seccomp}

Traditional Unix permissions attach owner, group, and other access rules
to filesystem objects. Credentials determine which class applies.
The superuser model historically provided broad administrative authority.
These mechanisms support multiuser isolation, but they are not a complete
answer to mutually hostile applications sharing one user's account.

An ordinary desktop application may be able to read many of that user's
files, connect to local services, and access inherited credentials.
If it is compromised, the damage can extend well beyond its intended task.
The principle of least privilege asks that a component receive only the
authority it actually needs.

Privilege separation can divide a service into a small component that
performs restricted privileged operations and larger components running
with less authority. Linux capabilities split some traditional root powers;
mandatory-access-control systems such as SELinux and AppArmor impose
additional policies; seccomp can restrict system-call behavior.
Each mechanism has a defined scope and can be undermined by mistakes in
the surrounding configuration.

Chroot changes a process's apparent filesystem root, but it is not by
itself a complete isolation boundary. Filesystem views are only one part
of a process's authority. Open descriptors, credentials, kernel attack
surface, and other interfaces remain relevant. A historical convenience
must not be promoted into a security guarantee it was not designed to make.

\section{Names and resources are different problems}
\index{namespaces}\index{cgroups}

Linux namespaces can give processes different views of selected system
resources: process identifiers, mounts, networking, and other domains.
A process inside a container can see a filesystem layout and PID space
that differ from the host's ordinary view. The kernel is still shared.

Control groups organize processes for resource control and accounting.
They can constrain or weight use of CPU, memory, I/O, and other resources,
depending on controllers and configuration. A namespace can hide a name;
a cgroup can constrain consumption. Confusing these functions makes it
hard to explain why a container can look isolated while still exhausting
a shared resource.

The cgroup v2 documentation describes a unified hierarchy and explicit
rules for controller behavior and delegation. It is a reminder that a
resource-control interface is itself a substantial system, with constraints
that administrators and runtimes must respect \parencite{cgroups}.
A value written to a control file is meaningful only within that model.

Containers assemble these and other mechanisms with filesystem images,
credentials, security policy, and runtime setup. They do not create a
second independent kernel. A virtual machine normally adds a guest kernel
behind a virtualized hardware boundary. The two approaches can coexist;
a cloud deployment may run containers inside virtual machines.

The distinction has practical consequences. A kernel vulnerability can
affect containers sharing that kernel. A virtual machine has its own
attack surface in the hypervisor and device model. The right boundary
depends on the threat model and operational requirements, not on a universal
claim that one packaging format is always safe.

\section{An image is not the running service}

A container image can record a filesystem and execution metadata.
It does not necessarily include external data, secrets, network policy,
host kernel behavior, or the service's complete deployment configuration.
Reproducing an image is therefore not the same as reproducing the service.

Persistent data requires a separate lifecycle. Replacing an application
container should not accidentally discard the database, and rolling back
an executable may not roll back a schema change. Versioned artifacts
help, but applications must define compatibility across transitions.
The most expensive failures often occur between versions rather than
within a single version.

Orchestrators such as Kubernetes coordinate desired state across many
machines. They can place work, restart it, expose services, and manage
rollouts. Their control loops add another layer of asynchronous behavior.
The fact that a configuration has been accepted does not mean every
machine has already reached the requested state.

This resembles the old distinction between runnable and running.
A declaration of intent and an observed condition are different facts.
Good operators measure the latter, while keeping enough history to
understand why the control system has not achieved the former.

\section{Configuration is a program with delayed consequences}

Shell scripts remain common in infrastructure because they can orchestrate
existing tools with little ceremony. Greenberg and colleagues observe that
cloud environments have not displaced the shell; they have supplied more
places for it to appear \parencite{shell-future}.
The danger is treating a script as disposable after it becomes essential.

Configuration languages have their own semantics.
Defaults, inheritance, includes, variable expansion, and merge rules can
create behavior no individual file states plainly. A system can contain
a hidden state machine in the interaction among scripts, service managers,
and remote controllers even when no source file calls it one.

Declarative configuration helps when the desired state is well specified
and convergence is controlled. It does not guarantee harmless repetition.
An operation described as a resource may still trigger irreversible work.
A migration, credential rotation, or storage deletion needs its own
transition rules and recovery plan.

The Unix tradition offers a useful discipline here: make inputs explicit,
check status, keep diagnostic output distinct from machine data, and
preserve observable intermediate state. These practices are valuable
whether the final controller is a shell script or a large service.

\section{Reliability is a maintenance relationship}
\index{observability}\index{updates}\index{backups}

A reliable service needs monitoring, logs, updates, tested restoration,
and people who understand its failure modes. A backup that has never been
restored is evidence that data was copied, not proof that service can be
recovered. A health check that merely confirms a process exists can miss
a service unable to answer useful requests.

Metrics, logs, and traces provide different evidence.
A metric can reveal a rising error rate; a log can explain an individual
failure; a trace can connect work across services. None should require
exposing secrets or collecting irrelevant personal data to be useful.
Observability is the design of evidence, not an invitation to record
everything indiscriminately.

Updates have a similar dual role. They repair defects while introducing
change. Long-term maintenance may preserve an interface while backporting
security fixes. A version string alone may not tell whether a known defect
is present. Operators need the distributor's actual patch and support
information.

The supplied random-input reliability study remains relevant as a method,
not as a current statistic. Frequently used components can contain
unexplored failure paths. Fuzzing, fault injection, and recovery exercises
supply evidence ordinary successful operation cannot provide
\parencite{fuzz}.

\section{Infrastructure preserves the old questions at new scale}

The server and cloud eras did not abolish Unix's fundamental concerns.
Processes still need lifetimes, names still need interpretation,
communication still has partial failures, and persistent state still
needs crash semantics. Automation multiplies the speed and reach of both
good decisions and mistakes.

The major change is that the person affected by a failure may be far from
the person who can diagnose it. Clear contracts and useful evidence become
more valuable as that distance grows. The system's success is measured
less by how elegantly it can be configured than by how predictably it
serves people who never see the configuration.
